style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.160      General
site plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.170      Homeowners'
association.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.180      Improvements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.190      Innocent
purchaser.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.200      Land
surveyor.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.205      Large lot
segregation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.210      Lot.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.215      Microsubdivision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.220      Nonbuilding
lot.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.230      Ownership
interest.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.240      Parent
parcel.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.250      Plat,
final.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.260      Plat,
preliminary.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.270      Revisions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.280      Segregation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.290      Short
plat, final.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.300      Short
plat, preliminary.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.310      Short
subdivision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.320      Subdivision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.04.330      Tract.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:103.5pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-103.5pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.010  Acre.</b>  Acre:  an area of
land equal to forty-three thousand, five hundred sixty square feet. (Ord. 13694
§ 3, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.020  Alteration.</b>  Alteration: 
the modification of a previously recorded plat, short plat, binding site plan,
or any portion thereof, that results in changes to conditions of approval, the
addition of new lots or more land, or the deletion of existing lots or the
removal of plat or lot restrictions or dedications that are shown on the
recorded plat.  (Ord. 17191 § 8, 2011:  Ord. 13694 § 4, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.030  Applicant.</b>  Applicant:  a
property owner, a public agency, or public or private utility that owns a
right-of-way or other easement or has been adjudicated the right to such
easement in accordance with RCW 8.08.040, or any person or entity designated or
named in writing by the property or easement owner to be the applicant, in an
application for a development proposal, permit, or approval.  (Ord. 19881 § 33,
2024:  Ord. 13694 § 5, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.040  Binding site plan.</b>  Binding
site plan:  a plan drawn to scale processed in accordance with *K.C.C.
19A.16.080 through 19A.20.040 and chapter 58.17 RCW.  (Ord. 13694 § 6, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><i><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></i></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>*Reviser's note:  The reference to &quot;sections
68 through 73 of this ordinance,&quot; codified as K.C.C. 19A.16.080 through
19A.20.040, appears to be erroneous.  Reference to K.C.C. 19A.20.010 through
19A.20.060 was apparently intended.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.050  Building envelope.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Building envelope:  the area of
a lot that delineates the limits of where a building may be placed on a lot. 
(Ord. 13694 § 7, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.060  Building site.</b>  Building
site: an area of land, consisting of one or more lots or portions of lots, that
is:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Capable of being developed under current
federal, state, and local statutes, including zoning and use provisions,
dimensional standards, minimum lot area, minimum lot area for construction,
minimum lot width, shoreline master program provisions, critical area
provisions and health and safety provisions; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Currently legally developed.  (Ord. 15031 §
4, 2004:  Ord. 13694 § 8, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.070  Civil engineer.</b>  Civil
engineer:  an individual registered and licensed as a professional civil
engineer pursuant to chapter 18.43 RCW.  (Ord. 13694 § 9, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.080  Condominium.</b>  Condominium: 
real property, portions of which are designated for separate ownership and the
remainder of which is designated for common ownership solely by the owners of
those portions as defined in chapters 64.32 and 64.34 RCW.  Real property is
not a condominium unless the undivided interests in the common elements are
vested in the unit owners and unless a declaration, survey map and plans have
been recorded pursuant to chapter 64.32 or 64.34 RCW.  (Ord. 13694 § 11, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.082  Condominium declaration.</b> 
Condominium declaration:  the document that creates a condominium by setting
forth the information required by chapters 64.32 and 64.34 RCW, as applicable,
including the survey map and plans, and that is recorded in conjunction with a
condominium survey map and plans.  (Ord. 16950 § 1, 2010).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.090  Dedication.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Dedication:  the deliberate
conveyance of land by an owner for any general and public uses, reserving no
rights other than those that are compatible with the full exercise and
enjoyment of the public uses for which the property has been conveyed.  The intention
to dedicate shall be evidenced by the owner by the presentment for filing of a
final plat, short plat or binding site plan showing the dedication thereon or
quit claim deed.  The acceptance by the public shall be evidenced by the approval
of such plat, short plat, binding site plan or quit claim deed for filing by
the county.  (Ord. 13694 § 12, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.100  Department.</b>  Department: 
the King County department of local services or its successor.  (Ord. 18791 § 139,
2018:  Ord. 17420 § 76, 2012:  Ord. 13694 § 13, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.110  Development engineer.</b> 
Development engineer:  the department of local services permitting division
manager or designee authorized to oversee the review, conditioning, inspection
and acceptance of right-of-way use permits, road and drainage projects
constructed pursuant to permits administered by the department and required
pursuant to this title.  The designee shall be a professional civil engineer
registered and licensed pursuant to chapter 18.43 RCW.  (Ord. 18791 § 140,
2018:  Ord. 18683 § 31, 2018:  Ord. 17420 § 77, 2012:  Ord. 13694 § 14, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.120  Director.</b>  Director:  the department
of local services permitting division manager or designee.  (Ord. 18791 § 141,
2018:  Ord. 18683 § 32, 2018:  Ord. 17420 § 78, 2012:  Ord. 13694 § 15, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.130  Easement.</b>  Easement:  a
right granted by a property owner to specifically named parties or to the
public for the use of certain land for specified purposes, that may include,
but are not limited to, road access, pedestrian or bicycle pathways, minerals,
utility easements, drainage and open space.  (Ord. 13694 § 16, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.140  Engineered preliminary drainage
plan.</b>  Engineered preliminary drainage plan:  a preliminary plan,
consistent with the King County Surface Water Design Manual, that shows the
locations, types and approximate sizes of the proposed drainage and conveyance
facilities, including any required bioswales, wetponds or other water quality
facilities. (Ord. 13694 § 10, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.150  Financial guarantee.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Financial guarantee:  a form of
financial security posted to ensure timely and proper completion of
improvements, compliance with the King County Code or to warrant materials, and
quality of work of the improvements and design.  Financial guarantees include
assignments of funds, cash deposits, surety bonds and other forms of financial
security acceptable to the director. (Ord. 18683 § 33, 2018:  Ord. 13694 § 17,
1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.160  General site plan.</b>  General
site plan:  a site plan approved pursuant to this title that is not based on a
recorded final planned unit development, a building permit, an as-built site
plan for developed sites or a site development permit issued for the entire
site.  (Ord. 13694 § 18, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.170  Homeowners’ association.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Homeowners’ association:  any
combination or grouping of persons or any association, corporation or other
entity that represents homeowners residing in a short subdivision, subdivision
or binding site plan.  A homeowners’ association need not have any official
status as a separate legal entity under the laws of the state of Washington. 
(Ord. 13694 § 19, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.180  Improvements.</b>  Improvements:
constructed appurtenances, including but not limited to road and drainage
construction, utility installation, recreational features, lot grading prior to
a building permit, plat monument signs, survey monuments.  (Ord. 13694 § 20,
1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.190  Innocent purchaser.</b> 
Innocent purchaser: an individual who has purchased real property for value and
states under oath that the individual had no knowledge at any time prior to or
during the sale that the lot had been or is being created in violation of the
provisions of this title.  (Ord. 18683 § 34, 2018:  Ord. 13694 § 21, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.200  Land surveyor.</b>  Land
surveyor:  an individual licensed as a land surveyor pursuant to chapter 18.43
RCW.  (Ord. 13694 § 22, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.205 
Large lot segregation.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  &quot;Large lot segregation&quot;
means the division of land into lots or tracts each one of which is
one-sixteenth of a section of land or larger, or forty acres or larger if the
land is not capable of description as a fraction of a section of land.  However,
for purposes of computing the size of a lot that borders on a street or road,
the lot size shall be expanded to include that area that would be bounded by
the center line of the road or street and the side lot lines of the lot running
perpendicular to such center line.  Also, within the resource zones, each lot
or tract shall be of a size that meets the minimum lot size requirements of
K.C.C. Title 21A for the respective zone.  (Ord. 19881 § 35, 2024:  Ord. 17841
§ 1, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.210  Lot.</b>  Lot:  a physically
separate and distinct parcel of property that has been created pursuant to the
provisions of this title, or pursuant to any previous state or local laws
governing the subdivision, short subdivision or segregation of land.  (Ord.
17191 § 9, 2011:  Ord. 13694 § 23, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.215  Microsubdivision.</b></span>  <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Microsubdivision:  a short
subdivision involving a division or redivision of land into two lots, tracts,
parcels, or sites for the purpose of the sale, lease, or transfer of ownership. 
(Ord. 19881 § 34, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.220  Nonbuilding lot.</b> 
Nonbuilding lot:  a lot created defined as a nonbuilding lot on the face of the
plat or short plat, for which improvements for the purpose of human habitation
or occupancy are prohibited.  (Ord. 13694 § 24, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.230  Ownership interest.</b> 
Ownership interest:  having property rights as a fee owner, contract
purchaser.  (Ord. 13694 § 25, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.240  Parent parcel.</b>  Parent
parcel: each existing lot that is located within the perimeter of a proposed
boundary line adjustment application.  (Ord. 13694 § 35, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.250  Plat, final.</b>  Final plat: 
the final drawing of the subdivision and dedication prepared for filing with
the county auditor and containing all elements and requirements set forth in
this title and in chapter 58.17 RCW.  (Ord. 13694 § 26, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.260  Plat, preliminary.</b> 
Preliminary plat:  a neat and approximate drawing of a proposed subdivision
showing the general layout of streets, alleys, lots, blocks and other elements
of a subdivision required by this title and chapter 58.17 RCW.  The preliminary
plat shall be the basis for the approval or disapproval of the general layout
of a subdivision.  (Ord. 13694 § 27, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.270  Revisions.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Revisions:  a change prior to final
approval or recording of a previously approved preliminary plat, preliminary
short plat or binding site plan that includes, but is not limited to, the
addition of new lots, tracts or parcels.  (Ord. 17191 § 10, 2011:  Ord. 13694 §
28, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.280  Segregation. </b> Segregation: 
a division of land by any of the following means: subdivisions, short
subdivisions, binding site plans and divisions described in K.C.C. 19A.04.040. 
(Ord. 13694 § 29, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.290  Short plat, final.</b>  Final
short plat:  the final drawing of the short subdivision and dedication prepared
for filing with the county auditor and containing all elements and requirements
set forth in this title and in chapter 58.17 RCW.  (Ord. 13694 § 30, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.04.300  Short plat, preliminary.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Preliminary short plat:  a neat
and approximate drawing of a proposed short subdivision showing the general
layout of streets, alleys, lots, blocks and other elements of a short
subdivision required by this title and chapter 58.17 RCW.  The preliminary
short plat shall be the basis for the approval or disapproval of the general
layout of a subdivision.  (Ord. 13694 § 31, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.310  Short subdivision.</b>  Short
subdivision:  inside the Urban Growth Area, a division or redivision of land
into nine or fewer lots, tracts, parcels, or sites for the purpose of the sale,
lease, or transfer of ownership.  Outside the Urban Growth Area, a division or
redivision of land into four or fewer lots, tracts, parcels, or sites for the
purpose of sale, lease, or transfer of ownership.  A microsubdivision is a type
of short subdivision.  (Ord. 19881 § 36, 2024:  Ord. 14788 § 1, 2003:  Ord.
13694 § 32, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.320  Subdivision.</b>  Subdivision:  outside
the Urban Growth Area, a division or redivision of land into five or more lots,
tracts or parcels for the purpose of sale, lease or transfer of ownership;
inside the Urban Growth Area, a division or subdivision of land into ten or
more lots, tracts or parcels for the purpose of sale, lease or transfer of
ownership.  (Ord. 14788 § 2, 2003:  Ord. 13694 § 33, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.04.330  Tract.</b>  Tract:  land
reserved for specified uses including, but not limited to, reserve tracts,
recreation, open space, sensitive areas, surface water retention, utility
facilities and access.  Tracts are not considered lots or building sites for
purposes of residential dwelling construction.  (Ord. 13694 § 34, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc396901437"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>19A.08         ADMINISTRATION</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.010      Scope of
chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.020      Adverse
possession lawsuit – consent or judgment required.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.030      Transfer
of land or granting of an easement to a public agency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.040      Exemptions
– subdivision and short subdivision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.045      Limitations
in closed basins.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.050      Recording
map and legal descriptions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.060      Review
for conformity with other codes, plans and policies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.065      Lot
segregation, boundary line adjustment or condominium-related
approval-certification that property taxes are not delinquent required.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.070      Determining
and maintaining legal status of a lot.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.080      Removing
limitations on nonbuilding lots.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.090      Determining
innocent purchaser status.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.100      Public
street rights-of-way.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.110      Limitations
within future road corridors.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.120      Affidavit
of correction.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.130      Vertical
and horizontal survey controls.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.140      Financial
guarantees.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.150      Application
requirements for preliminary plats, preliminary short plats and preliminary binding
site plans.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.160      Minimum
improvements before final recording of plat or short plat - exceptions – post
of financial guarantee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.170      Violations
and enforcement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.180      Circumvention
of zoning density prohibited.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.08.190      Rules.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.010  Scope of chapter.</b>  This
chapter contains provisions general to the administration of land segregation. 
Any segregation of land is subject to the provisions of this title except as
stated herein.  (Ord. 13694 § 36, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.020  Adverse possession lawsuit –
consent or judgment required.</b>  Applications for segregation allowed by this
title concerning lands on which there is a pending lawsuit for adverse
possession will not receive final approval without the consent of the adverse
possession claimant or until a trial court judgment settling the lawsuit is
entered.  (Ord. 13694 § 37, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.030  Transfer of land or granting of
an easement to a public agency.</b>  The transfer of land or granting of an
easement to a public agency for road and utility purposes shall not be
considered a segregation of land.  (Ord. 13694 § 38, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.040  Exemptions – subdivision and
short subdivision.</b> </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  The subdivision and short subdivision
provisions of this title shall not apply to large lot segregations.  A lot
created through a large lot segregation may not be further segregated for a
period of five years from the date of approval of the large lot segregation
unless it is subdivided in accordance with K.C.C chapter 19A.12.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The short subdivision provisions of this
title shall not apply to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>             1.  Divisions of land into lots or tracts
only for the purpose of allowing fee simple purchase or deeding of such lots or
tracts to public agencies; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Divisions of land by a public roadway
or freeway, as defined by the King County Roadway Functional Classification
System, that is planned, established, financed and constructed by a state or
county agency after January 1, 2000.  (</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17841 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>2, 2014</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>:  Ord. 17539 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>§ 9, 2013:  <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 13694 § 39, 1999).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.045  Limitations in closed basins.</b></span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>In a closed
basin, as defined by chapters 173-507, 173-503, 173-509, 173-510 and 173-515
WAC, an application for further segregation may not be submitted within five
years after recording, if the application relies on a public water system
created to provide domestic water that uses an exempt well under RCW 90.44.050
or proposes an additional exempt well and the proposed segregation will result
in the creation of more than six lots within the boundaries of the original
subdivision or short subdivision.  (Ord. 15031 § 1, 2004).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.08.050  Recording map and legal
descriptions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
The final recording map and legal description of a plat, short plat, boundary
line adjustment or binding site plan shall be prepared by a land surveyor in
accordance with chapter 58.09 RCW and chapter 332-130 WAC, Surveys and
Recording, and be recorded with the records and licensing services division as
required by this title.  (Ord. 15971 § 85, 2007:  Ord. 13694 § 40, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.060  Review for conformity with other
codes, plans and policies.</b>  Applications for approvals under this title
shall be reviewed in accordance with the applicable procedures of any
combination of this title and K.C.C. chapters 20.20 and 20.22.  Furthermore,
applications for subdivisions, short subdivisions and binding site plans may be
approved, approved with conditions or denied in accordance with the following
adopted county and state rules, regulations, plans and policies including, but
not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Chapter 43.21C RCW (SEPA);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Chapter 58.17 RCW (Subdivisions);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Chapters 36.70A and 36.70B RCW (Growth
Management and Project Review);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  K.C.C. Title 9 (Surface Water Management);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  K.C.C. Title 13 (Sewer and Water);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  K.C.C. Title 14 (Roads and Bridges);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  K.C.C. Title 17 (Fire Code);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          H.  K.C.C. chapter 20.44 (SEPA);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          I.   K.C.C. Title 21A (Zoning);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          J.  K.C.C. Title 23 (Code Enforcement);</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          K.  Administrative rules adopted under K.C.C.
chapter 2.98;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          L.   King County board of health rules and
regulations;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          M.  King County approved utility comprehensive
plans;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          N.  King County Comprehensive Plan;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          O.  Countywide Planning Policies; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          P.  This title.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(<span style='color:black'>Ord. 18230 § 107, 2016:  </span>Ord.
17539 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>§ 10, 2013:  <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 13694 § 41, 1999).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.08.065  Lot segregation, boundary
line adjustment or condominium-related approval-certification that property
taxes are not delinquent required.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Before final approval of any lot segregation, boundary
line adjustment or condominium-related approval under this title, the applicant
shall provide the department with a certification from the manager of the
finance and business operations division that property taxes for the subject
property are not delinquent.  (Ord. 17823 § 41, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.070  Determining and maintaining
legal status of a lot.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  A property owner may request
that the department determine whether a lot was legally created.  The property
owner shall demonstrate to the satisfaction of the department that a lot was
created in compliance with applicable state and local land segregation statutes
or codes in effect at the time the lot was created. </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  A lot shall be recognized as a legal lot:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  If before October 1, 1972, it was:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.  conveyed as an individually described
parcel to separate, noncontiguous ownerships through a fee simple transfer or
purchase; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  recognized as a separate tax lot by the
county assessor;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  If created by a recorded subdivision before
June 9, 1937, and it was served by one of the following before January 1, 2000:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.  an approved sewage disposal; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  an approved water system;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  If created by an approved short
subdivision, including engineers subdivisions;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  If created by a recorded subdivision on
or after June 9, 1937; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  If created through the following
alternative means of lot segregation provided for by state statute or county
code:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.  at a size five acres or greater, created
by a record of survey recorded between August 11, 1969, and October 1, 1972,
and that did not contain a dedication;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  at a size twenty acres or greater, created
by a record of survey recorded before January 1, 2000, and not subsequently
merged into a larger lot;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              c.  at a size forty acres or greater
created  through a larger lot segregation made in accordance with RCW 58.18.010,
approved by King County and not subsequently merged into a larger lot.  Within
the F zone, each lot or tract shall be of a size that meets the minimum lot
size requirements of K.C.C. 21A.09T.030;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              d.  through testamentary provisions or the
laws of descent after August 10, 1969; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              e.  as a result of deeding land to a public
body after April 3, 1977. </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  In requesting a determination, the property
owner shall submit evidence, deemed acceptable to the department, such as:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Recorded subdivisions or division of land
into four lots or less;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  King County documents indicating approval
of a short subdivision;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Recorded deeds or contracts describing
the lot or lots either individually or as part of a conjunctive legal
description, </span> <span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>such
as Lot 1 and Lot 2; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Historic tax records or other similar
evidence, describing the lot as an individual parcel.  The department shall
give great weight to the existence of historic tax records or tax parcels in
making its determination.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Once the department has determined that the
lot was legally created, the department shall continue to acknowledge the lot
as such, unless the property owner reaggregates or merges the lot with another
lot or lots in order to: </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Create a parcel of land that would
qualify as a building site, or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Implement a deed restriction or
condition, a covenant or court decision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  The department’s determination shall not be
construed as a guarantee that the lot constitutes a building site as defined in
K.C.C. 19A.04.060.  Testamentary lots created after December 31, 1999, and
before January 1, 2019, are exempt from meeting the minimum lot area
requirements in K.C.C. Title 21A for the applicable zone, if all other federal,
state and local statutes and regulations are met.  All other testamentary lots
shall be required to meet all federal, state and local statutes and
regulations, including minimum lot area requirements in K.C.C. Title 21A.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Reaggregation of lots after January 1, 2000,
shall only be the result of a deliberate action by a property owner expressly
requesting the department for a permanent merger of two or more lots through a
boundary line adjustment under K.C.C. chapter 19A.28.  (Ord. 19881 § 37, 2024: 
Ord. 19010 § 1, 2019:  Ord. 18764 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>§ 1,
2018:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17539 </span>§ 11, 2013:  <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17191 § 11, 2011:  Ord. 16687 § 1, 2009:  Ord.
15031 § 2, 2004:  Ord. 13694 § 42, 1999).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.080  Removing limitations on
nonbuilding lots.</b>  Limitations placed on a nonbuilding lot may be removed
and the lot recognized by King County as a building lot by approval of a
subdivision, short subdivision, binding site plan or alteration of a plat,
short plat or binding site plan. (Ord. 13694 § 43, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.090  Determining innocent purchaser
status.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  An innocent purchaser of a
parcel divided in violation of King County subdivision requirements who files a
notarized affidavit of innocent purchase with the department on forms approved
by the director may seek to establish the parcel’s eligibility for county
development approvals and for lawful future conveyance; provided that nothing
herein is intended to exempt development on innocent purchaser lots from
compliance with development standards of the county’s zoning code.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  All contiguous parcels divided in violation
of this title that are under common ownership at the time of application for
innocent purchaser status shall be recognized only as a single lot.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Innocent purchaser status shall not be
granted to any individual or group more than once.  (Ord. 13694 § 44, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.100  Public street rights-of-way.</b> 
Dedication or deeding to the county of right-of-way or a portion thereof for
public streets shall be required within or along the boundaries of all binding
site plans, subdivisions and short subdivisions or of any lot or lots within
them, under the following circumstances, where facts support that such
dedication is reasonably necessary as a result of the impact created by the
proposed development:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Where the six-year capital improvement plan
or transportation needs report indicates the necessity of a new right-of-way or
portion thereof for street purposes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Where necessary to extend or to complete
the existing or future neighborhood street pattern;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Where necessary to provide additions of
right-of-way to existing county right-of-way;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Where necessary to comply with county road
standards and King County road plans;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Where necessary to provide a public
transportation system that supports future development of abutting property
consistent with the King County Comprehensive Plan or King County zoning code,
provided that the right-of-way shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Provide for vehicular and pedestrian
circulation within and between neighborhoods;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Provide local traffic alternatives to the
use of arterial streets; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Reduce potential traffic impacts to
existing residential access streets.  (Ord. 13694 § 45, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.110  Limitations within future road
corridors.</b>  In order to allow for the development of future road corridors
that would complete the public circulation system or that would provide a sole
source of access for an abutting property, the county may limit improvements
within specific areas of a proposed binding site plan, subdivision or short
subdivision.  These limitations may preclude the construction of buildings,
driveways, drainage facilities or other improvements within the specified
areas.  (Ord. 13694 § 46, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.120  Affidavit of correction.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Any map page or document recorded with the
records and licensing services division, or its successor agency, under the
provisions of this title that contains an error in fact or omission may be
amended by an affidavit of correction.  The following types of errors may be
corrected by affidavit:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Any courses, distances or elevations
omitted from the recorded document;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.   An error in any courses, distances or
elevations shown on the recorded document;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  An error in the description of the real
property shown on the recorded document;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  An error in the field location of any
shown easement; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  Any other error or omission where the
error or omission is ascertainable from the data shown on the recorded
document.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Nothing in this section shall be construed
to permit changes in courses, distances or elevations for the purpose of
redesigning lot or tract configurations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  The affidavit of correction shall contain
the seal and signature of the land surveyor making the correction.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  The affidavit of correction shall set forth
in detail the corrections made and show the names of the present fee owners of
the property materially affected by the correction.  The notarized signatures
of the owners shall be required, if deemed necessary by the department.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  The affidavit of correction form, as
provided by the department, shall be submitted to the department for review and
approval and shall include signatures of the development engineer, the director
of the department, the assessor and the manager of the records and licensing
services division, or its successor agency.  After department approval, the
affidavit shall be recorded with the records and licensing services division,
or its successor agency. Submittals shall include payment of fees as authorized
by K.C.C. Title 27.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Should a nonsurvey-related error occur on
the recorded document as a result of information required to be placed on the
document by the department, the department’s responsible land surveyor may
prepare the affidavit providing the original land surveyor has no objections.
The seal and signature of the department’s responsible land surveyor making the
correction shall be affixed to the affidavit.  A copy of the affidavit shall be
mailed by the department to the original land surveyor following recording.  (Ord.
15971 § 86, 2007:  Ord. 14176 § 3, 2001:  Ord. 13694 § 47, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.130  Vertical and horizontal survey
controls.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  Vertical requirements.  The
vertical datum on all engineering plans, plats, binding site plans and short
plats shall be the North American vertical datum of 1988 and shall [be] tied to
at least one King County Survey Control Network benchmark.  The benchmark will
be shown on the plans.  If a King County Survey Control Network benchmark does
not exist within one-half mile of the subject property, or two hundred fifty
feet or greater of total vertical difference exists between the starting
benchmark and the project, an alternate vertical datum may be used.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Horizontal requirements.  The horizontal
component of all plats, binding site plans and short plats shall have the North
American datum of 1983/91 as its coordinate base and basis for bearings.  All
horizontal control for these projects shall be referenced to a minimum of two
King County Survey horizontal control monuments.  If two horizontal control
monuments do not exist within one mile of these projects, an alternate
coordinate base and basis of bearings may be used.  (Ord. 13694 § 48, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.140  Financial guarantees.</b> 
Notwithstanding any other provision of this title, the director is authorized
to require all applicants issued permits or approvals under the provisions of
this title to post financial guarantees consistent with the provisions of
K.C.C. Title 27A.  (Ord. 13694 § 49, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.150  Application requirements for
preliminary plats, preliminary short plats and preliminary binding site plans. </b> The
following application requirements shall be required in addition to those
application requirements described in K.C.C. 20.20.040:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  A title report issued within thirty days of
application, showing all persons having an ownership interest, a legal
description describing exterior boundary of application site and listing all
encumbrances affecting the site.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  A map prepared by a land surveyor showing
the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Location of all physical and legal
description encroachments affecting the boundary between the application site
and the adjoining parcels.  Encroachments may be from the application site onto
the adjoining parcels or from the adjoining parcels onto the application site;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Contours based upon topographic field
survey.  For land inside the urban growth boundary, contour intervals shall be
at two-foot intervals when slopes are fifteen percent or less and five-foot
intervals for slopes exceeding fifteen percent.  The preliminary map shall
contain notes indicating that contours are based upon field survey.  A field
topographic base map shall accompany the application.  If approved by the
department, field survey may be waived for large areas of open space or
extensive sensitive area tracts.  Two temporary benchmarks must be shown within
the application site along with the appropriate elevation and datum;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  A legal description of application site
as shown in the title report;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  The proposed layout of lots, tracts,
right-of-way and easements, along with existing utilities and areas of proposed
dedications;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  The purpose of any tracts and dedications
proposed within the application site;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            6.   All easements, listed in the title
report, capable of being plotted on the map;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            7.  Field-verified survey of location of all
known sensitive areas including, but not limited to, streams, wetlands and
steep slopes that may affect the proposal.  Show the approximate one
hundred-year floodplain of sensitive areas, where applicable;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            8.  Name of proposal;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            9.  North arrow, scale and date of map and
revisions when applicable;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            10.  Location of adjoining parcels and
buildings within one hundred feet of the site shall be shown and delineated by
dashed lines.  The zoning of the parcels shall also be identified;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            11.  Name and location of all existing
adjoining right-of-way along with the name and location of any adjoining or
internal right-of-way proposed to be vacated with the proposal;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            12.  A vicinity map; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            13.  An engineered preliminary drainage plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  A proposed binding site plan shall be
deemed to have satisfied the requirements of K.C.C. 19A.08.150B when the
binding site plan is based on a recorded final planned unit development,
building permit, as-built site plan for developed sites or a site development
permit for the entire site.  (Ord. 13694 § 50, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.08.160  Minimum improvements before
final recording of plat or short plan - exceptions – post of financial
guarantee.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Except as otherwise provided in subsection
B. of this section, before final recording of a plat or short plat, the
following minimum improvements shall be constructed consistent with the
approved plans;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Drainage facilities and erosion control
measures consistent with K.C.C. 9.04.090;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Water mains and hydrant installed and
fire flow available, sewer mains, laterals and sewer maintenance holes
installed, if required;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Roadways meeting the approved engineering
plan’s layout drainage, geometric and road width requirements and finished with
an asphalt treated base.  The final surfacing on the roadways may be bonded;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Pedestrian facilities complying with the
Americans with Disabilities Act; including but not limited to, curb ramps,
sidewalks and shoulders, where required;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  Specific site improvements required by
the preliminary plat approval ordinance or preliminary short plat approval
decision, if the decision requires completion before plat recording;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            6.  Delineation of sensitive areas that are
to remain undeveloped;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            7.  Temporary control monuments set by a land
surveyor, located in conformance with this title, and in place at final
inspection.  Permanent monuments and control points shall be set and verified
by a land surveyor within ninety days of the final lift of asphalt;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            8.  Improvements without which the director
determines a safety hazard would exist; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            9.  All private improvements outside of the
right-of-way or road easement and access tracts.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The director, in consultation with the
department of natural resources and parks, the department of local services,
road services division, the prosecuting attorney and other affected agencies,
may allow the applicant to post a financial guarantee for any identified
noncritical required improvements, as determined on a project by project basis,
if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  The expiration of the plat or short plat
is imminent or other extraordinary circumstances prevent the construction of
the improvements before the final recording;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  The inability to construct the
improvements is due to unavoidable circumstances that in no way resulted from
the actions or inaction of the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  The applicant submits a detailed
construction completion timeline and the department determines the applicant
will be able to complete the work or improvements to be covered by the
financial guarantee within a reasonable amount of time; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Approval of the final plat or short plat
before completion of the work or improvement will not be materially detrimental
to existing county infrastructure or private properties in the vicinity of the
subject property.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  The director shall have right of entry onto
any lot, tract, easement or parcel that is part of the final plat or short plat
to ensure compliance with the minimum subdivision improvements required in
subsection A. of this section.  (Ord. 18791 § 142, 2018:  Ord. 18683 § 35,
2018:  Ord. 17539 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>§ 12, 2013:  <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16267 § 10, 2008:  Ord. 13694 § 51, 1999).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.170  Violations and enforcement.</b> 
Any person or entity who violates this title or who sells or transfers a lot,
tract or parcel that was not created consistent with this title or chapter
58.17 RCW or that has not been recognized by the department as a legal lot
under this chapter shall, in addition to any remedies and sanctions provided
for under state law, be subject to the enforcement provisions of K.C.C. Title
23.  (Ord. 16267 § 9, 2008:  Ord. 13694 § 52, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.08.180  Circumvention of zoning
density prohibited.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>  A legal lot, which has been subject to a boundary line adjustment or
created through a legally recognized land segregation process and is of
sufficient land area to be subdivided at the density applicable to the lot, may
be further segregated.  However, such further segregation of the lot shall not
be permitted if the total number of lots contained within the external
boundaries of the lots subject to the original boundary line adjustment or the
total number of lots contained within the external boundary of the parcel
subject to the original land segregation, exceed the density allowed under
current zoning.  (Ord. 13694 § 53, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.08.190  Rules.</b>  The director is
authorized to adopt rules to implement the provisions of this title pursuant to
K.C.C. chapter 2.98.  (Ord. 13694 § 54, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc396901438"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>19A.12         SUBDIVISIONS
AND SHORT SUBDIVISIONS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.12.010      Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.12.020      Preliminary
approval of subdivision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.12.030      Revision
of preliminary subdivision, preliminary short subdivision, or binding site
plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.12.040      Preliminary
short subdivision – approval time.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.12.050      Limitations
for short subdivisions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.12.010  Purpose.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The purpose of this chapter is
to specify requirements for the segregation of land into short subdivisions and
subdivisions, in accordance with applicable Washington state and King County
laws, rules and regulations, including permit processing procedures required by
K.C.C. chapter 20.20.  (Ord. 14788 § 3, 2003:  Ord. 13694 § 55, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.12.020  Preliminary approval of
subdivision.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Preliminary subdivision approval shall be
effective for a period of sixty months.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Preliminary subdivision approval shall be
considered the basis upon which the applicant may proceed toward development of
the subdivision and preparation of the final plat subject to all the conditions
of the preliminary approval.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  If the subdivision is being developed in
divisions, and final plats for all of the divisions have not been recorded
within the time limits provided in this section, preliminary subdivision
approval for all unrecorded divisions shall become void.  The preliminary
subdivision for any unrecorded divisions shall be submitted again to the
department with a new application, subject to the fees and regulations
applicable at the time of submittal.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  An applicant for a preliminary subdivision
approved on or after January 1, 2015, who files a written request for extension
with the director at least thirty days before the expiration of the preliminary
subdivision, shall be granted a one-time, one-year extension dated from the
original preliminary approval date. Any plat not recorded within the time set
forth in this subsection is null and void and the applicant is required to
resubmit a new preliminary subdivision for approval, subject to all current regulations. 
(Ord. 19881 § 38, 2024:  Ord. 17191 § 12, 2011:  Ord. 16593 § 1, 2009:  Ord.
16515 § 2, 2009:  Ord. 15055 § 2, 2004:  Ord. 14747 § 2, 2003:  Ord. 14113 § 2,
2001:  Ord. 13694 § 56, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.12.030  Revision of preliminary
subdivision, preliminary short subdivision, or binding site plan.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  A request to revise a preliminary
subdivision, preliminary short subdivision, or binding site plan that has received
preliminary approval shall be submitted to the department.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Proposed revisions to a preliminary
subdivision that would result in a substantial change, as defined in K.C.C.
20.20.100, shall be treated as a new application for purposes of vesting and transportation
concurrency and shall be reviewed as Type 3 land use decision under K.C.C.
20.20.020.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Proposed revisions to a preliminary short
subdivision or binding site plan that would result in a substantial change, as defined
in K.C.C. 20.20.100, shall be treated as a new application for purposes of
vesting and, where applicable, transportation concurrency, and shall be
reviewed as Type 2 land use decision under K.C.C. 20.20.020, except that a
proposed revision to a microsubdivision in the urban area shall be reviewed as
a Type I land use decision under K.C.C. 20.20.020.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Proposed changes to a preliminary
subdivision, preliminary short subdivision, or binding site plan that do not
result in a substantial change, as defined in K.C.C. 20.20.100, shall be
treated as a minor change and may be approved administratively by the
department.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  For purposes of this section, minor changes
include, but are not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1. Changes to engineering design standards
necessitated by changed circumstances, such as reconfiguration or reduction of
lots;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Changes in lot dimensions that are
consistent with the underlying zone;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  A decrease in the number of lots to be
created so long as the minimum lot size and minimum density of the underlying
zone is maintained;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Changes in timing of phased plans; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  Changes to engineering design that reduce
construction related impacts and do not eliminate off-site improvements
specifically required as a condition of preliminary approval.  (Ord. 19881 § 39,
2024:  Ord. 19872 § 12, 2024:  Ord. 17191 § 13, 2011:  Ord. 13694 § 57, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.12.040</b>  <b>Preliminary short
subdivision - approval time.</b>  Preliminary approval of a short subdivision
shall be effective for a period of sixty months, except:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  The approval period shall be eighty-four
months for any short plat that was part of a development agreement or
interlocal agreement entered into after January 1, 1996, that included at least
four hundred acres of open space dedications and urban land designations at a
four-to-one ratio; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.1.  For any short plat that has received
preliminary short approval on or after December 1, 2003, the preliminary short subdivision
approval shall be valid for a period of eighty-four months.  The department may
make revisions to the fee estimate issued by the department under K.C.C.
27.02.065*.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  This subsection shall retroactively apply
to any short plat that has received preliminary approval on or after December
1, 2003.  This subsection expires December 31, 2014.  (Ord. 17191 § 14, 2011:  Ord.
16593 § 2, 2009:  Ord. 16515 § 3, 2009:  Ord. 14788 § 4, 2003: Ord. 13694 § 58,
1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>*Reviser's note:  K.C.C. 27.02.065 was replaced by
Ordinance 17682, Section 50.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.12.050  Limitations for short subdivisions.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Inside the Urban Growth Area, a maximum of
nine lots may be created by a single application.  Outside the Urban Growth
Area, a maximum of four lots may be created by a single application.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  An application for further segregation may
not be submitted within five years after recording, except through the filing
of a subdivision application or unless the short plat contains fewer than nine
lots inside the Urban Growth Area or fewer than four lots outside the Urban
Growth Area, in which case an alteration application may be submitted to create
a cumulative total of up to nine lots inside the Urban Growth Area or up to four
lots outside of the Urban Growth Area within the original short plat boundary.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  A maximum of nine lots inside the Urban
Growth Area or eight lots outside the Urban Growth area may be created from two
or more contiguous parcels with any common ownership interest.  (Ord. 15031 § 4,
2004:  Ord. 14788 § 5, 2003:  Ord. 13694 § 59, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc396901439"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>19A.16         FINAL
PLAT AND FINAL SHORT PLAT MAPS FOR PRELIMINARILY APPROVED SUBDIVISIONS AND
SHORT SUBDIVISIONS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.010   Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.020   Phased
development.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.030   Final
plat and final short plat review procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.040   Final
plat and final short plat engineering plan review requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.050   Contents
of final plat and final short plat.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.055   Final
plat approval – duties of director.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.060   Final
forms.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.070   Alterations
of final plats.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.080   Alterations
of final short plats.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.090   Vacations
of plats, short plats, or county roads.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.010  Purpose.</b>  The purpose of
this chapter is to specify provisions that must be satisfied prior to the final
approval and recording of final plat and final short plat maps, for
preliminarily-approved subdivisions and short subdivisions.  (Ord. 13694 § 61,
1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.020  Phased development.</b> 
Portions of an approved preliminary subdivision may be processed separately by
the department for the purpose of recording divisions.  All divisions shall be
approved within the prescribed time limits for the preliminary subdivision, and
all conditions of approval for each particular division must be met.  (Ord.
13694 § 62, 1999). </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.030  Final plat and final short plat
review procedures.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Following submittal of the engineering
plans, a final plat or final short plat shall be surveyed by a land surveyor
and submitted to the department for review and approval by the development
engineer before recording.  If more than one sheet is required, an index sheet
shall be included that must show the entire segregation with road names and lot
numbers.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  All final plats and final short plats shall
conform to the conditions of preliminary approval.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Plat certificates or owner’s duplicate
certificates for land registered pursuant to chapter 65.12 RCW shall be
provided to the department before recording along with a copy of the last real
estate transaction for all adjoining unplatted parcels.  Supplemental plat
certificates shall be provided to the department if the final plat or final
short plat is not recorded within thirty days of the original certificate or
supplemental certificate date.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  All applicable processing fees specified by
K.C.C. Title 27 and any civil penalty assessed pursuant to K.C.C. Title 23
against a site being reviewed under this section shall be paid prior to
recording;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  A deposit to cover anticipated taxes and
assessments is required for final plats pursuant to chapter 58.08 RCW.  A
deposit, however, shall not be required for the filing of a final short plat.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Proof of sewer and water availability,
including any required water rights, shall be submitted to the department and
final health department approval shall be obtained before recording, if
applicable.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  Upon approval by the department, the final
plat or short plat shall be recorded with the records and licensing services
division.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          H.  A typewritten copy of protective deed
covenants shall accompany the final plat or short plat, if applicable.  (Ord.
17823 § 8, 2014:  Ord. 15971 § 87, 2007:  Ord. 13694 § 63, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.040  Final plat and final short plat
engineering plan review requirements.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Engineering plans for roads, drainage
controls and other proposed or conditioned improvements shall be prepared,
submitted and reviewed for approval by the development engineer prior to the
commencement of on-site clearing or construction activities.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Approval of the engineering details of the
proposed sanitary sewer and water systems and other proposed public facilities
by the development engineer and the King County department of public health
will be required prior to the approval of the final plat.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Plans and technical information reports
shall be submitted to the department and prepared consistent with the
requirements of the King County Road Standards, K.C.C. chapter 14.42, Surface
Water Design Manual, K.C.C. chapter 9.04 and conditions of preliminary
approval.  Each plan set or document shall be stamped, signed and dated by a
civil engineer.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Prior to approval of engineering plans, the
applicant shall post a site restoration guarantee consistent with the
provisions of K.C.C. Title 27A, and shall pay all applicable fees set forth in
K.C.C. Title 27. (Ord. 13694 § 64, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.050  Contents of final plat and final
short plat.</b>  The following information shall be shown on a final plat or
final short plat:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Name of subdivision and department file
number for final plats or department file number for final short plats;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Location by section, township and range,
and by legal description;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  The signature and seal of the land
surveyor;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Survey map requirements as specified in
chapter 332-130 WAC and chapter 58.09 RCW;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Boundary of plat or short plat based on
relative accuracy procedures or field traverse standards, and meeting or
exceeding those standards specified in WAC 332-130-090;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Exact location, width and name of all
streets within and adjoining the plat or short plat, and the exact location and
widths of all alleys.  The naming of a street shall conform to the county’s
process for naming streets;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  Courses and distances to the nearest
established street lines or official monuments that shall accurately describe
the location of the plat or short plat;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          H.  Municipal, township, county or section
lines accurately tied to the lines of the plat or short plat distances and
courses;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          I.  All easements for rights-of-way provided
for public utilities;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          J.  Lots designated by number on the plat or
short plat within the area of the lot, and tracts similarly designated by
letter.  Each tract shall be clearly identified with the ownership, purpose and
maintenance responsibility;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          K.  Blocks in numbered additions to plats
bearing the same name may be numbered or lettered consecutively through the
several additions;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          L.  Accurate location of all existing and
proposed permanent control monuments at each corner of the subdivision or short
subdivision consistent with RCW 58.17.240 and at all road intersections and
curve control points that fall within the pavement;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          M.  A traverse line established along the shore
not more than twenty feet landward of the ordinary high water mark when a
subdivision or short subdivision borders on a body of water.  This line shall
be labeled “Plat traverse line” or “Short plat traverse line”, as applicable,
on the final plat or short plat documents;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          N.  Accurate boundary delineation for any areas
to be dedicated or reserved for public use, along with the purposes of the use
indicated thereon; and the accurate delineation of any areas to be reserved by
deed covenant for common uses of all property owners;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          O.  The boundary description of the property
being platted or short platted matching the description recorded in the most
recent real estate transfer document encompassing the property.  If the
description is incorrect, a true and exact description shall be shown upon the
plat or short plat together with the original description. The original
description shall be labeled “original description” and the true and exact
description shall be labeled “surveyor’s corrected description.”  The
surveyor’s corrected description shall be preceded by the verbiage:  “The
intent of the original description is to encompass all of the property
described within the surveyor’s corrected description”;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          P.  Dedication with notarized acknowledgments
by all parties having an ownership interest, as required by RCW 58.17.165 and K.C.C.
19A.04.230, acknowledging the adoption of the plat and the dedication of
streets and other public areas.  Dedications by corporations shall include
corporate acknowledgment and dedications by individuals shall include
individual acknowledgment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          Q.  Restrictions, title encumbrances and notes
required by the conditions of approval;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          R.  Certification by a land surveyor to the
effect that the plat or short plat correctly represents a survey made by the
surveyor, or under the surveyor’s direction, and that the existing monuments
are located as shown on the final plat or final short plat;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          S.  Approval and signature blocks for the
department, the department of assessments and the finance and business
operations division;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          T.  Recording certificate required for the
signature of the records and licensing services division. (Ord. 18710 § 1, 2018: 
Ord. 15971 § 88, 2007:  Ord. 13694 § 65, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.055 
Final plat approval – duties of director.</b></span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          </span></b>A. 
As authorized by and subject to the requirements of chapter 58.17 RCW, the
council hereby delegates final plat approval to the director.</p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>          B.  Before approval, the
director shall certify that:</p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>            1. Appropriate
provisions are made for the public health, safety and general welfare and for
such open spaces, drainage ways, streets or roads, alleys, other public ways,
transit stops, potable water supplies, sanitary wastes, parks and recreation,
playgrounds, schools and school grounds and all other relevant facts, including
sidewalks and other planning features that assure safe walking conditions for
students who only walk to and from school;</p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>            2.  The public use and
interest will be served by the subdivision or dedication, or both; and</p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>            3. The final plat is
consistent with applicable laws, regulations and adopted policies, including,
but not limited, to the requirements of this title and chapter 58.17 RCW.</p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>          C. 
Before recording, the director shall send electronic notice of the department's
final plat approval to the councilmember in whose district the project is
located and the clerk of the council, who shall retain the original email and
provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the council chief of staff,
the policy staff director and the lead staff for the transportation, economy
and environment committee, or its successor.  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(Ord. 18710 § 2, 2018).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.060  Final forms.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  A final plat or final short
plat shall be prepared on forms eighteen inches by twenty-four inches in size,
allowing for a two-inch border on one of the eighteen-inch sides, to allow for
binding, and one-half-inch borders on the other three sides.  The two-inch
border will typically be on the top or left side depending on the configuration
of the drawing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Forms shall be printed with materials
acceptable for filing as specified in WAC 332-130-050 and be formatted
consistent with forms provided by the department.  (Ord. 13694 § 66, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.070  Alterations of final plats.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  Alterations shall be
processed in accordance with RCW 58.17.215 through 58.17.218 and shall comply
with regulations in effect at the time the alteration application was
submitted.  Alteration applications and recording documents shall contain the
signatures of the majority of those persons having an ownership interest in
lots, tracts, parcels or divisions in the subject subdivision to be altered or
any portion to be altered.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  If the subdivision is subject to
restrictive covenants that were filed at the time of the approval of the
subdivision, and the application for alteration would result in the violation
of a covenant, the application shall contain an agreement signed by all parties
subject to the covenants providing that the parties agree to terminate or alter
the relevant covenants to accomplish the purpose of the alteration of the
subdivision or portion thereof.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Notice of alterations shall comply with the
notice provisions of K.C.C. Title 20.  Mailing notification shall also include
owners of each lot or parcel of property within the subdivision to be altered.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  An application shall be processed as a Type
3 permit pursuant to K.C.C. chapter 20.20 and K.C.C. 20.22.040.  The
application may be approved if the proposed alteration is consistent with the
required findings of K.C.C. 20.22.180.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  After approval of an alteration, the
applicant shall produce a revised drawing of the approved alteration of the
final plat, to be processed in the same manner as set forth for final plats in
this title.  (<span style='color:black'>Ord. 18230 § 108, 2016:  </span>Ord.
13694 § 67, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.16.080  Alterations of final short
plats.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
Alteration of a final short plat may be approved by the department when
consistent with the following requirements:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Alterations shall be accomplished by
following the same procedure and satisfying the same laws, rules and conditions
as required for a new short plat application, as set forth in this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Alteration applications and recording
documents shall contain the signatures of the majority of those persons having
an ownership interest in lots, tracts, parcels or divisions in the subject
short plat to be altered or any portion to be altered.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  If the short subdivision is subject to
restrictive covenants that were filed at the time of the approval of the short
subdivision, and the application for alteration would result in the violation
of a covenant, the application shall contain an agreement signed by all parties
subject to the covenants providing that the parties agree to terminate or alter
the relevant covenants to accomplish the purpose of the alteration of the short
subdivision or portion thereof.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Notice of alterations shall comply with the
notice provisions of K.C.C. Title 20.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  An alteration may be allowed to remove
nonbuilding lot status on short subdivisions provided that no public
dedications are required and original conditions of approval do not prohibit
conversion of a nonbuilding lot to a building lot.  Approval of such alteration
requires completion of the original conditions of approval, and the application
of new conditions for the lot, consistent with current standards, preparation
of a new map page prepared by a land surveyor for recording and payment of all
fees required for such review. (Ord. 13694 § 68, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.16.090  Vacations of plats, short plats,
or county roads.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  Plat vacations shall be
processed as follows and in accordance with RCW 58.17.212.  All plat vacation
applications shall be reviewed as Type 3 decisions in accordance with K.C.C.
20.20.020.  The hearing examiner must determine that the proposed vacation is
consistent with the required findings of K.C.C. 20.22.180</span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>to approve the vacation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Short plat vacations shall be processed in
accordance with RCW 58.17.060.  All short plat vacation applications shall be
reviewed as Type 2 decisions in accordance with K.C.C. 20.20.020.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Applications for vacations of county roads
may be processed under this chapter only when such road vacations are proposed
in conjunction with the vacation of the plat.  Vacations limited to county
roads shall be processed in accordance with chapter 36.87 RCW</span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>and K.C.C. chapter 14.40.  (Ord.
19648 § 8, 2023:  <span style='color:black'>Ord. 18230 § 109, 2016:  </span>Ord.
13694 § 69, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc396901440"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>19A.20         BINDING
SITE PLANS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.20.010      Purposes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.20.020      Applicability.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.20.030      Requirements
and limitations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.20.040      Alterations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.20.050      Vacations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.20.060      Recording.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.20.010  Purposes.</b>  The purposes of
this chapter are:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  To provide an alternative method for
division of land as authorized by RCW 58.17.035;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  To ensure through covenants, conditions,
restrictions, easements and other requirements binding upon all lot owners that
the collective lots continue to function as one site concerning, but not
limited to, public roads, improvements, open spaces, drainage and other
elements specified in this chapter;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  To allow the director to authorize sharing
of open space, parking, access and other improvements among contiguous
properties subject to the binding site plan; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  To specify administrative requirements for
binding site plans in addition to the procedural requirements of K.C.C. chapter
20.20 and in accordance with applicable Washington state and King County laws,
rules and regulations.  (Ord. 16950 § 2, 2010:  Ord. 13694 § 70, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.20.020  Applicability.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.1.  The division of commercial or industrial
zoned land for sale or lease when used for commercial or industrial purposes,
or the division of land for lease when used as a mobile home park or
recreational vehicle park; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  The division of land resulting from
subjecting a portion of a parcel or tract to the Horizontal Property Regimes
Act, chapter 64.32 RCW, or the Condominium Act, chapter 64.34 RCW.  After
approval of a binding site plan for land, all or a portion of which will be
subjected to the provisions of chapter 64.32 or 64.34 RCW.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The applicant shall record the approved
binding site plan with the King County recorder’s office.  Following
recordation of the binding site plan, the applicant shall submit to the
department for review a condominium declaration, survey map and plans as
required by chapters 64.32 and 64.34 RCW.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  A binding site plan for a condominium shall
be based on a recorded final planned unit development, a building permit, an
as-built site plan for developed sites or a site development permit issued for
the entire site or a general site plan showing the anticipated development plan
for the entire site.  As determined by the department, binding site plan
reviews may take place independently for developed sites or concurrently with
or subsequent to a building permit or site development permit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  The site that is subject to the binding
site plan shall consist of one or more contiguous lots.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  The site that is subject to the binding
site plan may be reviewed independently for developed sites, concurrently with
or subsequent to a site development permit application for undeveloped land or
concurrently with or subsequent to a building permit application.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  The binding site plan process creates or
alters lot lines and does not authorize substantial improvements or changes to
the property or the uses thereon.  (Ord. 16950 § 3, 2010:  Ord. 13694 § 71,
1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.20.030  Requirements and limitations.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  The binding site plan shall
ensure that the collective lots continue to function as one site with respect
to, but not limited to, lot access, interior circulation, open space,
landscaping, drainage facilities, facility maintenance and parking.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The binding site plan shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Identify the areas and locations of all
streets, roads, improvements, utilities, open spaces, sensitive areas, parking
areas, landscaped areas, surveyed topography for preliminary map, water bodies
and drainage features and building envelopes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Contain inscriptions or attachments
setting forth such limitations and conditions for the use of the land as are
established by the director or the hearing examiner; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Contain provisions requiring any
development or division of land to be in conformance with the approved site
plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Conditions of use, maintenance and
restrictions on redevelopment of shared open space, parking, access and other
improvements shall be identified and enforced by covenants, easements or other
similar mechanisms.  (Ord. 13694 § 72, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.20.040  Alterations.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  Alteration of a binding site
plan shall be accomplished by following the same process required for a new
application as set forth in this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Changes to a building permit, planned unit
development, subdivision or short subdivision, site development permit within a
binding site plan area shall also require alteration of the binding site plan
unless the director determines that such changes are consistent with the
approved binding site plan.  (Ord. 13694 § 73, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.20.050  Vacations.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  Vacation of a binding site
plan shall be accomplished by following the same procedure and satisfying the
same laws, rules and conditions as required for a new binding site plan
application, as set forth in this chapter.  If a portion of a binding site plan
is vacated, the property subject to the vacated portion shall constitute one
lot unless the property is subsequently divided by an approved subdivision or
short subdivision or another binding site plan.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  If a building permit or commercial site
development permit is revised or expires, then the binding site plan shall be
vacated unless the director determines that the revision or expiration is
consistent with the approved binding site plan. (Ord. 13694 § 74, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          19A.20.060  Recording.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  Plat certificates or owner’s
duplicate certificates for registered land pursuant to chapter 65.12 RCW shall
be provided to the department by the owner along with a copy of the last real
estate transaction for all adjoining unplatted parcels.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Before recording, the approved binding site
plan shall be surveyed and the final recording forms shall be prepared by a
land surveyor.  A final binding site plan shall be prepared on forms eighteen
inches by twenty-four inches in size, allowing for a two-inch border on one of
the eighteen-inch sides, to allow for binding, and one-half-inch borders on the
other three sides.  The two-inch border will typically be on the top or left
side depending on the configuration of the drawing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  The approved binding site plan recording
documents shall include the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Except for a binding site plan for a
condominium, identification of lots by number on a binding site plan containing
more than one lot. Tracts shall be similarly designated and each tract shall be
clearly identified with the ownership and purpose;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Signature and stamp of the land surveyor
who prepared the binding site plan in accordance with chapter 332-130 WAC and
chapter 58.09 RCW;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Reference to the recording number of the
completed survey if the boundaries have been previously surveyed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Reference to all agreements or covenants
required as a condition of approval;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  Notarized signatures of all parties
having an ownership interest in the land being divided;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            6.   Satisfaction of health department
requirements, unless previously approved on a recorded final planned unit
development, a building permit, an as-built plan for developed sites or a site
development permit for the entire site;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            7.  Approval of the King County development
engineer;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            8.  Approval of the King County finance and
business operations division;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            9.  Approval of the King County assessor; </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            10.  Approval of the director;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            11.  Recording certificate required for
signature of the records and licensing services division; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            12.  Department file number.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  A deposit to cover anticipated taxes and
assessments is required for binding site plans pursuant to chapter 58.08 RCW.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Lots, parcels or tracts created through the
binding site plan procedure shall be legal lots of record. All provisions,
conditions and requirements of the binding site plan shall be legally
enforceable on the purchaser or any other person acquiring a lease or other
ownership interest of any lot, parcel, or tract created pursuant to the binding
site plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  No person shall sell, transfer or lease of
any lot, tract or parcel created pursuant to the binding site plan that does
not conform to the requirements of the binding site plan or without binding
site plan approval.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  The binding site plan shall set forth
limitations and conditions, including irrevocable dedications of property and
containing a provision that any development of the site shall be in conformity
with the approved binding site plan.  (Ord. 17823 § 9, 2014:  Ord. 15971 § 89,
2007:  Ord. 13694 § 75, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc396901441"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>19A.24         CONDOMINIUMS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.24.010      Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.24.020      Final
submittal requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.24.030      Notes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.24.010  Purpose.</b>  The purpose of
this chapter is to provide for review of condominiums and condominium
declarations to ensure compliance with chapters 64.32 and 64.34 RCW.  The
review shall include, but is not limited to, the review of a condominium survey
map and plans for the precision and accuracy of the exterior boundary and legal
description of the subject property, as shown on the final map.  In accordance
with RCW 64.34.050(1), the review shall not impose any requirement upon a
condominium that would not be imposed upon a physically identical development
under a different form of ownership.  (Ord. 16950 § 4, 2010:  Ord. 13694 § 76,
1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.24.020  Final submittal requirements.</b> 
The following shall be submitted for approval of a condominium proposal:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Two sets of prints of the final recording
maps prepared in accordance with RCW 64.34.232;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Legal Description from title report dated
within thirty days prior to recording;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Boundary closure calculations and
supporting surveys; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Copy of last real estate transaction for
all adjoining unplatted parcels.  (Ord. 13694 § 77, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.24.030  Notes.</b>  An approval block
for the department or its successor in substantially the following form shall
be added to the recording document: </span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          &quot;Approval of the Department of Local
Services, Permitting Division:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          Examined and Approved this ____ day of
__________, 2____.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          Manager, Permitting Division&quot;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(Ord. 18791 § 143, 2018:  Ord. 17420 § 79, 2012:  Ord.
16950 § 5, 2010:  Ord. 13694 § 78, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc396901442"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>19A.28         ADJUSTMENTS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.28.010      Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.28.020      Procedures
and limitations of the boundary line adjustment process.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.28.030      Final
approval and recording required.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        19A.28.040      Conceptual
review.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.28.010  Purpose.</b>  The purpose of
this chapter is to provide procedures and criteria for the review and approval
of adjustments to boundary lines of legal lots or building sites in order to
rectify defects in legal descriptions, to allow the enlargement or merging of
lots to improve or qualify as a building site, to achieve increased setbacks
from property lines or sensitive areas, to correct situations wherein an
established use is located across a lot line, or for other similar purposes.  (</span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17841 </span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>3, 2014</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>:  Ord. 13694 § 79, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.28.020  Procedures and limitations of
the boundary line adjustment process.</b>  Adjustment of boundary lines between
adjacent lots shall be consistent with the following review procedures and
limitations:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Applications for boundary line adjustments
shall be reviewed as a Type 1 permit as provided in K.C.C. chapter 20.20.  The
review shall include examination for consistency with the King County zoning
code, K.C.C. Title 21A., shoreline master program regulations, applicable board
of health regulations, and, for developed lots, fire and building codes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  A lot created through a large lot
segregation shall be consistent with the underlying zoning and shall not be
reduced to less than twenty acres within ten years of the large lot segregation
approval unless it is subdivided in accordance with K.C.C. chapter 19A.12;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.   Transfer of property ownership between
adjacent legal lots shall not occur until the boundary line adjustment is
approved;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  A boundary line adjustment proposal shall
not:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Result in the creation of an additional
lot;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Result in the creation of more than one
additional building site in the rural area and natural resource lands or two
additional building sites in the urban area;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Result in a lot that does not qualify as
a building site under this title;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Relocate an entire lot from one parent
parcel into another parent parcel;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  Reduce the overall area in a plat or
short plat devoted to open space;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            6.  Be inconsistent with any restrictions or
conditions of approval for a recorded plat or short plat;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            7.  Involve lots that do not have a common
boundary;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            8.  Circumvent the subdivision or short
subdivision procedures in this title.  Factors that indicate that the boundary
line adjustment process is being used in a manner inconsistent with statutory
intent include: numerous and frequent adjustments to the existing lot boundary,
a proposal to move a lot or building site to a different location, and a large
number of lots being proposed for a boundary line adjustment; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            9.  Circumvent standards or procedures in
K.C.C. Title 21A;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  The elimination of lines between two or
more lots shall, in all cases, be considered a minor adjustment of boundary
lines and shall not be subject to the subdivision and short subdivision
provisions of this title or to K.C.C. 19A.28.030.  The format and requirements
of a minor adjustment under this subsection shall be specified by the department;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Recognized lots in an approved site plan
for a conditional use permit, special use permit, or commercial site
development permit shall be considered a single site and no lot lines on the
site may be altered by a boundary line adjustment to transfer density or
separate lots to another property not included in the original site plan of the
subject development; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  Lots that have been subject to a boundary
line adjustment process that resulted in the qualification of an additional
building site shall not be allowed to utilize the boundary line adjustment
process again for five years to create an additional building site.  (Ord.
19881 § 40, 2024:  </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>Ord. 17841 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§ </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>4,
2014</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>:  Ord. 17191 §
15, 2011:  Ord. 16950 § 6, 2010:  Ord. 13694 § 80, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.28.030</b>  <b>Final approval and
recording required.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.  A title insurance certificate
updated-not more than thirty days prior to recording of the adjustment, which
includes all parcels within the adjustment, must be submitted to the department
with boundary line adjustment final review documents.  All persons having an
ownership interest within the boundary line adjustment shall sign the final
recording document in the presence of a notary public.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Prior to final approval, documentation
authorizing the transfer of property ownership shall be placed on the original
boundary line map along with the legal descriptions of those portions of land
being transferred when lots are under separate ownership.  Lot lines within
lots under the same ownership will be adjusted upon the recording of the
boundary line adjustment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Final record-of-survey document must be
prepared by a land surveyor in accordance with chapter 332-130 WAC and chapter
58.09 RCW.  The document must contain a land surveyor’s certificate and a
recording certificate.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  The final map page shall contain the
following approval blocks:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  The King County department of assessments
to be signed by the King County assessor and deputy King County assessor; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  The department of local services,
permitting division, to be signed by the permitting division manager.  (Ord. 18791
§ 144, 2018:  Ord. 17420 § 80, 2012:  Ord. 13694 § 81, 1999).</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>19A.28.040  Conceptual review.</b>  Prior to
proceeding through the boundary line adjustment process, an applicant may
desire to pay for an optional preapplication meeting.  At that time the
department will perform a conceptual review based upon the information brought
in by the applicant.  (Ord. 13694 § 82, 1999).</span></p>

</div>

</body>

</html>
